{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_019yeD5Cjbkqasq1Aidc3iES","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Configuring Nginx as a Reverse Proxy\n2\t\n3\tSetting up Nginx as a reverse proxy is a straightforward process. By following these steps, you'll have a working configuration in minutes.\n4\t\n5\t### Installing Nginx\n6\t\n7\tBegin by installing Nginx on your server. Running the package manager will handle downloading and configuring the necessary dependencies:\n8\t\n9\t```bash\n10\tsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n11\t```\n12\t\n13\t### Creating the Configuration\n14\t\n15\tAfter completing the installation, you'll need to create a configuration file. Understanding the proxy directives is essential for achieving optimal results.\n16\t\n17\tCreate a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n18\t\n19\t```nginx\n20\tserver {\n21\t    listen 80;\n22\t    server_name myapp.example.com;\n23\t\n24\t    location / {\n25\t        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n26\t        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n27\t    }\n28\t}\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\t### Enabling and Testing\n32\t\n33\tEnabling the site requires creating a symbolic link. Leveraging Nginx's built-in testing capabilities ensures your configuration is valid before reloading:\n34\t\n35\t```bash\n36\tsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\n37\tsudo nginx -t\n38\tsudo systemctl reload nginx\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\tIn order to verify everything is working, try accessing your domain in a browser. The groundbreaking simplicity of this setup showcases how reverse proxying can streamline your deployment workflow.\n42\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"40aa53a2-058f-49e4-a8d8-9f648a0a508b","uuid":"84ab9e56-656a-4b6b-9451-89e0409f00ef","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:35:22.763Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-013-tutorial-ing-endings/source.md","content":"## Configuring Nginx as a Reverse Proxy\n\nSetting up Nginx as a reverse proxy is a straightforward process. By following these steps, you'll have a working configuration in minutes.\n\n### Installing Nginx\n\nBegin by installing Nginx on your server. Running the package manager will handle downloading and configuring the necessary dependencies:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n```\n\n### Creating the Configuration\n\nAfter completing the installation, you'll need to create a configuration file. Understanding the proxy directives is essential for achieving optimal results.\n\nCreate a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name myapp.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Enabling and Testing\n\nEnabling the site requires creating a symbolic link. Leveraging Nginx's built-in testing capabilities ensures your configuration is valid before reloading:\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\nIn order to verify everything is working, try accessing your domain in a browser. The groundbreaking simplicity of this setup showcases how reverse proxying can streamline your deployment workflow.\n","numLines":42,"startLine":1,"totalLines":42}}}
