{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01BczzHg4MEXDnab6zBDKtRi", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_vrtx_0116eweRGJ5cyCo2mZ6wGsGG", "name": "Edit", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-013-tutorial-ing-endings/source.md", "old_string": "## Configuring Nginx as a Reverse Proxy\n\nSetting up Nginx as a reverse proxy is a straightforward process. By following these steps, you'll have a working configuration in minutes.\n\n### Installing Nginx\n\nBegin by installing Nginx on your server. Running the package manager will handle downloading and configuring the necessary dependencies:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n```\n\n### Creating the Configuration\n\nAfter completing the installation, you'll need to create a configuration file. Understanding the proxy directives is essential for achieving optimal results.\n\nCreate a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name myapp.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Enabling and Testing\n\nEnabling the site requires creating a symbolic link. Leveraging Nginx's built-in testing capabilities ensures your configuration is valid before reloading:\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\nIn order to verify everything is working, try accessing your domain in a browser. The groundbreaking simplicity of this setup showcases how reverse proxying can streamline your deployment workflow.", "new_string": "## Configuring Nginx as a reverse proxy\n\nThis gets Nginx forwarding requests to a backend app. The whole thing takes about five minutes.\n\n### Install Nginx\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n```\n\n### Create the configuration\n\nAdd a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name myapp.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `proxy_pass` directive tells Nginx where to forward requests. `proxy_set_header Host` passes the original hostname through so your backend knows which domain was requested.\n\n### Enable and test\n\nLink the config into `sites-enabled`, then test before reloading:\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\n`nginx -t` checks for syntax errors without touching the running server. If it reports OK, the reload is safe. Hit your domain in a browser to confirm requests are reaching the backend."}}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 623, "cache_read_input_tokens": 39198, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 623, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "40aa53a2-058f-49e4-a8d8-9f648a0a508b", "uuid": "2f6d6dde-458c-4c47-a777-c144cea9f862", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T16:35:36.926Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0116eweRGJ5cyCo2mZ6wGsGG","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-013-tutorial-ing-endings/source.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"40aa53a2-058f-49e4-a8d8-9f648a0a508b","uuid":"0279292f-c6bb-435a-83cb-ecb254fa6f8c","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:35:37.023Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-013-tutorial-ing-endings/source.md","oldString":"## Configuring Nginx as a Reverse Proxy\n\nSetting up Nginx as a reverse proxy is a straightforward process. By following these steps, you'll have a working configuration in minutes.\n\n### Installing Nginx\n\nBegin by installing Nginx on your server. Running the package manager will handle downloading and configuring the necessary dependencies:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n```\n\n### Creating the Configuration\n\nAfter completing the installation, you'll need to create a configuration file. Understanding the proxy directives is essential for achieving optimal results.\n\nCreate a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name myapp.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Enabling and Testing\n\nEnabling the site requires creating a symbolic link. Leveraging Nginx's built-in testing capabilities ensures your configuration is valid before reloading:\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\nIn order to verify everything is working, try accessing your domain in a browser. The groundbreaking simplicity of this setup showcases how reverse proxying can streamline your deployment workflow.","newString":"## Configuring Nginx as a reverse proxy\n\nThis gets Nginx forwarding requests to a backend app. The whole thing takes about five minutes.\n\n### Install Nginx\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n```\n\n### Create the configuration\n\nAdd a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name myapp.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `proxy_pass` directive tells Nginx where to forward requests. `proxy_set_header Host` passes the original hostname through so your backend knows which domain was requested.\n\n### Enable and test\n\nLink the config into `sites-enabled`, then test before reloading:\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\n`nginx -t` checks for syntax errors without touching the running server. If it reports OK, the reload is safe. Hit your domain in a browser to confirm requests are reaching the backend.","originalFile":"## Configuring Nginx as a Reverse Proxy\n\nSetting up Nginx as a reverse proxy is a straightforward process. By following these steps, you'll have a working configuration in minutes.\n\n### Installing Nginx\n\nBegin by installing Nginx on your server. Running the package manager will handle downloading and configuring the necessary dependencies:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx\n```\n\n### Creating the Configuration\n\nAfter completing the installation, you'll need to create a configuration file. Understanding the proxy directives is essential for achieving optimal results.\n\nCreate a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name myapp.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Enabling and Testing\n\nEnabling the site requires creating a symbolic link. Leveraging Nginx's built-in testing capabilities ensures your configuration is valid before reloading:\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsudo nginx -t\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\nIn order to verify everything is working, try accessing your domain in a browser. The groundbreaking simplicity of this setup showcases how reverse proxying can streamline your deployment workflow.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":21,"newStart":1,"newLines":17,"lines":["-## Configuring Nginx as a Reverse Proxy","+## Configuring Nginx as a reverse proxy"," ","-Setting up Nginx as a reverse proxy is a straightforward process. By following these steps, you'll have a working configuration in minutes.","+This gets Nginx forwarding requests to a backend app. The whole thing takes about five minutes."," ","-### Installing Nginx","+### Install Nginx"," ","-Begin by installing Nginx on your server. Running the package manager will handle downloading and configuring the necessary dependencies:","-"," ```bash"," sudo apt update && sudo apt install nginx"," ```"," ","-### Creating the Configuration","+### Create the configuration"," ","-After completing the installation, you'll need to create a configuration file. Understanding the proxy directives is essential for achieving optimal results.","+Add a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:"," ","-Create a new file at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`:","-"," ```nginx"," server {","     listen 80;"]},{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":14,"newStart":24,"newLines":16,"lines":[" }"," ```"," ","-### Enabling and Testing","+The `proxy_pass` directive tells Nginx where to forward requests. `proxy_set_header Host` passes the original hostname through so your backend knows which domain was requested."," ","-Enabling the site requires creating a symbolic link. Leveraging Nginx's built-in testing capabilities ensures your configuration is valid before reloading:","+### Enable and test"," ","+Link the config into `sites-enabled`, then test before reloading:","+"," ```bash"," sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/"," sudo nginx -t"," sudo systemctl reload nginx"," ```"," ","-In order to verify everything is working, try accessing your domain in a browser. The groundbreaking simplicity of this setup showcases how reverse proxying can streamline your deployment workflow.","+`nginx -t` checks for syntax errors without touching the running server. If it reports OK, the reload is safe. Hit your domain in a browser to confirm requests are reaching the backend."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
